// VA mastery 1 — construction, one-time close, rehabilitation. Public sources as of 2026-10-26; verify against the current VA Lenders Handbook (VA Pamphlet 26-7) and lender overlays.
window.VA_TOPICS=(window.VA_TOPICS||[]).concat([
 {k:"va-construction",n:"VA construction loans",sum:"VA does not lend money; private lenders make and fund VA-guaranteed loans, and VA guarantees them. The VA Lenders Handbook (Chapter 7, Topic 2) allows both one-time-close construction/permanent loans (the VA guaranty covers the whole loan) and two-time-close structures (a non-VA interim construction loan taken out by a VA permanent loan). Very few lenders actually offer VA construction loans, and those that do layer heavy overlays, so the real skill is knowing which structure fits and which lender will really close it.",
  sec:[
   {h:"What actually exists (and the common misconception)",b:[
    "VA does not fund anything; it guarantees loans made by approved private lenders, so there is no such thing as VA itself paying a builder.",
    "The common claim that VA guarantees only the permanent loan is only half right: it describes the two-time-close structure, where a non-VA construction loan is paid off at completion by a VA purchase loan.",
    "VA Circular 26-18-7 (April 2018) replaced Lenders Handbook Chapter 7, Topic 2 and defined a one-time close loan as one used to close both the construction loan and the permanent financing at the same time, with draws funded from an escrow-type account.",
    "A two-time close is described in that circular as an initial loan closing before construction starts, then a second closing where VA permanent financing takes out or replaces the initial loan.",
    "Topic 2, Construction/Permanent Loans, was revised in its entirety in Change 43 to VA Pamphlet 26-7 (published June 2024; one lender-compliance source says effective June 5, 2024, another says no effective date was stated, so confirm the effective date with the AE).",
    "Change 43 highlights reported by industry sources: builder-paid versus borrower-paid costs, contingency fund requirements, lender acceptance of change orders, inspection requirements and project management.",
    "The circular was issued because the thin prior guidance kept many lenders out of the product; the practical result is that availability is still limited and is a lender decision, not a VA decision."]},
   {h:"Three ways a veteran can end up with a new home",b:[
    "Buy a completed or nearly completed spec/inventory home from a builder: this is an ordinary VA purchase of 'new construction' and is by far the easiest path.",
    "Buy a home to be built by a builder on the builder's lot (proposed construction): the veteran contracts with a builder and the lender closes either a one-time-close VA loan or a two-time-close sequence.",
    "Build on land the veteran already owns or is buying: lot-plus-construction on one VA loan if the lender offers it, or a non-VA construction loan followed by a VA refinance/permanent loan.",
    "A veteran who contracts directly to build is treated as a purchase transaction regardless of the label on the closing disclosure, and per a law-firm summary of the circular lenders must order the case as new construction and purchase in WebLGY.",
    "Using VA to buy a lot only (raw land with no home) is not a VA purchase purpose; the lot must be financed together with the construction or by other means (confirm with the lender)."]},
   {h:"New construction definitions that drive the paperwork",b:[
    "The Handbook (Sections 10.05 and 10.08 per a 2019 lender summary) separates 'proposed construction', 'under construction' and 'new construction'.",
    "A property counts as 'new construction' if it is fully completed or nearly completed with only customer-preference items left, per the lender summary of Chapter 10.",
    "New construction (appraised as such) must be covered by either a one-year VA builder's warranty or a ten-year insurance-backed protection plan, per Chapter 10.08 summaries.",
    "The one-year builder warranty is VA Form 26-1859, Warranty of Completion of Construction; VA will not guarantee the loan without the signed form in the file when the warranty route is used.",
    "A 2023 vendor note says the revised 26-1859 includes a certification that the property was built to HUD or VA requirements and that copies go to buyer and builder at closing.",
    "If the veteran acts as their own general contractor, vendors report the lender must obtain the veteran's signed acknowledgment that no construction warranty is provided (confirm in Chapter 10 and with the AE).",
    "A March 31, 2025 VA circular reportedly rescinded the requirement for a VA builder identification number on new and proposed construction loans and updated how builder complaints are handled; older guides still mention builder registration, so treat those as stale."]},
   {h:"Forms and the valuation chain",b:[
    "VA Form 26-1805, Request for Determination of Reasonable Value, is the request for appraisal (older forms index; confirm the current edition, as most lenders now order through VA's WebLGY/appraisal portal).",
    "VA Form 26-1843 is the Certificate of Reasonable Value in the older forms index; VA now issues a Notice of Value (NOV) from the appraisal, and the value on it governs the maximum loan.",
    "VA Form 26-1852 is the Description of Materials (the same content as HUD-92005): builders fill it for homes proposed to be bought with VA loans, and a technically qualified third party, not VA staff, reviews it against the IRC, local code and VA minimum property requirements (VA Federal Register information-collection notice, 2024).",
    "The appraiser uses the plans, specifications and Description of Materials to establish 'as-completed' value before the home exists.",
    "VA Form 26-1880 is the Request for a Certificate of Eligibility, not a construction document; if a checklist tells you 26-1880 belongs in the construction closing package, it is only the COE request.",
    "VA Form 26-1820 (Report and Certification of Loan Disbursement) is the closing report the lender submits after closing; confirm timing for construction loans with the AE."]},
   {h:"Appraisal, value and the NOV",b:[
    "For proposed construction the appraisal is an 'as-completed' valuation based on plans, specs, the contract and a cost breakdown, so the file must contain full plans and a complete Description of Materials before ordering.",
    "The NOV sets the reasonable value; VA loans cannot exceed it without the veteran paying the difference, and the veteran may not be pressured to sign if the value comes in low (VA gives the veteran the right to request a reconsideration of value).",
    "VA fee appraisers (not salaried staff) perform most appraisals; the lender selects the case through the VA appraisal system, and the VA staff appraisal reviewer role (SAR) is a VA-side quality review function that can add conditions, so confirm who issues the NOV in the current process.",
    "The appraisal flags minimum property requirement (MPR) issues; on proposed construction the MPRs are intended to make sure the home is built to the applicable code, federal regulations and HUD requirements (Chapter 12 summary).",
    "An appraiser can condition the NOV on completion 'per plans and specifications', which in a two-time close means the final permanent-loan appraisal or completion report must confirm that completion before the VA loan can close.",
    "Value is an appraisal question, not a contract-price question: a builder's upgrade-heavy price can exceed as-completed value, leaving the veteran with a cash gap."]},
   {h:"Inspections, completion and the warranty",b:[
    "Archived Chapter 14 text (a lender-hosted copy) said that for proposed/under-construction homes with an insured ten-year protection plan only a final (third-stage) inspection is required, otherwise a fuller series of inspections is required; however a VA circular states VA stopped conducting compliance inspections on new and proposed construction in February 2006, relying on local building inspections and 1-year or 10-year warranties. These conflict, so confirm with current Chapter 10 and 14 text.",
    "Whatever the VA role, the lender in a one-time-close loan owns the draw inspections and project management under Topic 2; expect a third-party inspector or the lender's own draw process before each disbursement.",
    "Completion requires a certificate of occupancy (or local equivalent), a final inspection/completion report, and for the warranty route a signed 26-1859 delivered at or before closing.",
    "A final walk-through and punch list should be completed before the veteran signs off on the last draw, because after the final draw leverage over the builder is gone.",
    "VA's warranty for a one-year builder warranty is the builder's promise, not VA's; if the builder is gone the veteran has no VA remedy beyond the complaint process."]},
   {h:"Funds flow, draws, interest and contingency",b:[
    "Under the circular, draws on a one-time close loan come from an escrow-type account and the lender must obtain the borrower's written approval before each disbursement.",
    "Interest during construction: the circular states the builder is responsible for interest during the construction period on a one-time close unless it is included in an interest reserve; on a two-time close the interim loan terms are negotiated.",
    "Contingency reserves are an allowable acquisition cost under the circular; the exact percentage and rules were revised in Change 43 and I could not confirm the current percentage, so lender overlay: confirm with the AE (one wholesale renovation guideline uses 15% for renovations, which is a different product).",
    "Change orders: the lender must accept them under Topic 2; any increase in cost generally needs funds from the veteran or builder and may need a value update, so insist on no change orders without written lender approval.",
    "Time limits: one lender site says a construction period of up to 12 months is common with extensions possible, but that is a lender statement, not VA's text (confirm in current Topic 2).",
    "Money held back: VA lenders are not allowed to let the veteran pay builder costs that the builder is supposed to bear (builder-paid versus borrower-paid costs is a Change 43 topic)."]},
   {h:"Fees, funding fee and entitlement",b:[
    "The 2018 circular allowed a lender construction fee of up to 2 percent of the loan (if at least 51 percent of proceeds is paid out during the progress of construction), in addition to the 1 percent origination charge; Change 43 revised the whole topic, so confirm the fee cap is unchanged before quoting it.",
    "The VA funding fee on a construction loan uses the same chart as a purchase: with less than 5 percent down it is 2.15 percent for first use and 3.3 percent for subsequent use; 5 to under 10 percent down is 1.5 percent; 10 percent or more down is 1.25 percent (current rates run through November 14, 2031 per VBA as reported by secondary sources).",
    "Veterans receiving VA disability compensation and certain others are exempt from the funding fee (38 USC 3729).",
    "Entitlement is used when the loan closes (one-time close) or when the permanent VA loan closes (two-time close); for a one-time close the full construction/permanent loan is one VA loan.",
    "Full-entitlement veterans have no VA loan limit since the Blue Water Navy Vietnam Veterans Act (2020); partial-entitlement veterans are subject to the county limit, 2026 baseline $832,750, high-cost up to $1,249,125 (secondary sources).",
    "VA allows the veteran to finance the funding fee into the loan; on a construction loan the lender decides how the fee is carried in the budget."]},
   {h:"Property requirements: energy, termite, soil, water and sewer",b:[
    "VA minimum property requirements (Chapter 12) apply to the finished home: safe, sanitary, structurally sound, with adequate heating, utilities and access.",
    "A wood-destroying insect inspection is not required on every VA loan; it is required where local practice or the appraiser's observation calls for it and for some construction cases, and one secondary source says the inspection is valid 90 days for closing.",
    "For private wells the lender needs a lab water test and, where applicable, shared well agreements; septic systems must operate without surfacing effluent or backup, and many lenders require a septic design approval from the local health authority for new builds.",
    "Soil conditions, drainage and flood-zone status are appraisal and MPR matters; a lot in a special flood hazard area needs flood insurance.",
    "Energy-efficiency: new homes are built to the local energy code; VA does not mandate a HERS rating in the sources I found, so any energy-certification requirement is a lender or builder matter (confirm).",
    "Access: the lot needs legal access via a public road or recorded easement and utilities available to the site."]},
   {h:"Builder concessions, seller-paid costs and lender reality",b:[
    "VA allows seller concessions up to 4 percent of the reasonable value for the veteran's closing costs, prepaids, funding fee payoff and judgments, and normal discount points are not counted against that cap (confirm exact definitions in Chapter 8).",
    "Builder concessions are common on new homes, so the contract should state which items are credits and which are price reductions; the appraiser may need to see them.",
    "Veterans United publicly states it does not make VA construction loans for new-home building and instead suggests a builder or local-lender construction loan followed by refinance, which is the norm for national retail lenders.",
    "Lender availability is thin and many that do offer it impose overlays such as higher minimum FICO, lower DTI, a required down payment, approved-builder lists, no veteran-as-builder and limited states, all lender overlay: confirm with the AE.",
    "Community banks, credit unions and a few wholesale lenders are the usual sources; ask the AE directly for the lender's construction manual and a written list of overlays."]},
   {h:"What can go wrong and how to avoid it",b:[
    "The appraisal comes in under cost because of upgrades or lot premiums: get an early value opinion or a plan-and-spec review before the veteran signs a final contract.",
    "The builder runs out of funds mid-project: vet the builder's financials, require lien waivers at each draw, and use title company draw control.",
    "Rate or program changes between contract and closing in a two-time close leave the veteran needing to requalify at permanent closing: lock early with an extended lock and watch credit and employment changes.",
    "A change order or upgrade after approval breaks the budget: require written change orders approved by the lender before work.",
    "Builder delays beyond the time limit or lock expiry: build lock extension costs and delay penalties into the contract.",
    "The veteran assumes a one-year warranty means VA backs it: explain the warranty is the builder's."]}
  ],
  steps:[
   "Qualify the lender first: ask the AE or lender whether it offers a VA one-time close or two-time close, what states, and get the written overlay list before any promise to the veteran.",
   "Pull the Certificate of Eligibility (request through VA, form 26-1880 or the online portal) and confirm entitlement: full versus remaining entitlement, prior VA loan, funding fee exemption.",
   "Pre-qualify under Chapter 4 underwriting: income, residual income, DTI, and the construction budget including any interest reserve and contingency.",
   "Decide the structure with the veteran: spec/inventory home purchase, builder one-time close, own-lot build, or a two-time close with a non-VA interim loan.",
   "Vet the builder: licenses, references, completed VA files, financials, whether the builder will give the one-year warranty (26-1859) or a ten-year insured plan, and confirm any current builder requirements since the 2025 builder ID rescission.",
   "Collect the contract, plans, specifications, Description of Materials (26-1852), cost breakdown and permit status.",
   "Order the as-completed appraisal; review the NOV for conditions and the reasonable value against the contract price.",
   "Set the budget: contract price, closing costs, funding fee (2.15 percent first use with no down payment), lock extension costs, interest reserve and contingency; decide who pays interest during construction.",
   "Lock the rate with an extended lock that matches the construction timeline, and document extension and float-down terms in writing.",
   "Close the loan, set up the construction escrow or interim loan, and collect the veteran's written approval process for each draw.",
   "Manage draws: inspection, lien waivers, borrower written approval, lender change-order approval.",
   "At completion collect the certificate of occupancy, final inspection/completion report, 26-1859 or the ten-year plan, and complete a final walk-through before the last draw.",
   "For a two-time close, order the permanent appraisal/completion report, requalify the veteran, close the VA loan and pay off the interim loan.",
   "Report the loan to VA after closing and keep the file complete for post-closing audit."],
  ex:[
   {t:"Builder purchase, $520,000 contract, first-time VA use, no down payment vs 5 percent down",rows:[
    ["Contract price","$520,000"],
    ["Zero down: base loan","$520,000"],
    ["Funding fee 2.15% x 520,000","$11,180"],
    ["Zero down: total loan","$531,180"],
    ["5% down payment (0.05 x 520,000)","$26,000"],
    ["5% down: base loan","$494,000"],
    ["Funding fee 1.5% x 494,000","$7,410"],
    ["5% down: total loan","$501,410"],
    ["Fee savings from 5% down","$3,770"]],
    note:"The total loan with zero down is about $29,770 larger than with 5 percent down, but the veteran keeps $26,000 of cash. Fee percentages are the current purchase/construction schedule; the funding fee rows exclude any lender construction fee."},
   {t:"Interest carry during construction on a $520,000 loan",rows:[
    ["Rate (illustrative)","6.5%"],
    ["Average drawn balance (about half)","$260,000"],
    ["Construction period","8 months"],
    ["Interest = 260,000 x 0.065 x 8/12","$11,267"],
    ["Who pays on a one-time close","Builder, unless included in interest reserve (circular 26-18-7)"]],
    note:"Use this to show the veteran why the contract must say who pays construction interest; if a lender pushes it onto the veteran it is real cash."}],
  q:[
   ["Does VA make construction loans?","No. Private lenders make them and VA guarantees; the loan can be a one-time close (VA guarantees the whole construction/permanent loan) or a two-time close with a non-VA interim loan."],
   ["Is it true VA guarantees only the permanent loan?","Only for the two-time-close structure. Chapter 7, Topic 2 also allows one-time-close construction/permanent loans."],
   ["Why can't my veteran find a lender?","Few lenders offer VA construction; large retail lenders such as Veterans United publicly say they do not. Try community banks, credit unions and specialized wholesale lenders, and ask for overlays in writing."],
   ["What warranty does a new VA home need?","Either a one-year builder warranty on VA Form 26-1859 or a ten-year insurance-backed plan, per Chapter 10 summaries."],
   ["Does the builder need a VA ID?","Reportedly not since a March 31, 2025 VA circular rescinded the builder ID requirement for new and proposed construction; confirm with the lender."],
   ["What is the funding fee on a construction loan?","Same as a purchase: 2.15 percent first use / 3.3 percent subsequent use under 5 percent down, 1.5 percent at 5 to under 10 percent, 1.25 percent at 10 percent or more; exempt for disability-compensated veterans."],
   ["Can the veteran be their own general contractor?","VA permits it per the circular summaries, but many lenders prohibit it as an overlay; if allowed, no builder warranty applies and the veteran signs an acknowledgment."],
   ["Who pays interest during construction?","On a one-time close, the builder unless covered by an interest reserve, per the circular. Put it in the contract."],
   ["Can the veteran put the lot equity in?","Often lender-specific; lot equity usually counts toward acquisition cost and may count as down payment, but confirm how the lender treats it."],
   ["How long is the construction period?","VA text is not confirmed here; lenders commonly allow about 12 months, with extensions possible. Lender overlay: confirm with the AE."],
   ["What if the appraisal is short?","The veteran may pay the difference in cash, renegotiate, or request a reconsideration of value with new comparables; VA does not exceed reasonable value."],
   ["Is there a VA loan limit?","Not for full-entitlement veterans. Partial entitlement uses the county limit (2026 baseline $832,750)."],
   ["Does a one-time close need a second appraisal?","Generally the as-completed appraisal supports the loan; a final completion inspection is required, but confirm whether a separate appraisal is required by the lender."]],
  caution:[
   "Most VA construction guidance online is lender marketing; the authoritative text is Chapter 7 Topic 2 (Change 43, 2024) and Chapters 10 and 12 of VA Pamphlet 26-7. Read them before quoting.",
   "Contingency percentages, time limits, construction fee caps and lot-equity treatment were not confirmed from primary text here: lender overlay, confirm with the AE.",
   "Conflicting sources exist on compliance inspections (archived Chapter 14 versus a VA circular saying VA stopped them in 2006); do not tell a veteran VA will inspect.",
   "Form numbers on older forms indexes (26-1805, 26-1843) are from 2004 snapshots; current ordering is through WebLGY and the VA issues an NOV.",
   "Do not use VA Form 26-1880 as a construction form; it is the COE request.",
   "A lender's overlays (FICO, DTI, down payment, builder list, no veteran-as-builder) can be stricter than VA; always get them in writing before taking an application fee.",
   "Rate-lock extension costs on long builds can be material (secondary sources cite 0.125 to 0.375 percent of the loan per extension), so quote them upfront.",
   "Seller or builder concessions over 4 percent of reasonable value are not allowed to be counted for the veteran; confirm what counts.",
   "Builder insolvency is the biggest practical risk; use title-controlled draws and lien waivers."],
  src:["https://www.benefits.va.gov/HOMELOANS/documents/circulars/26_18_7.pdf","https://www.benefits.va.gov/HOMELOANS/documents/circulars/26_18_6.pdf","https://www.tenaco.com/va-revises-topic-2-of-chapter-7-of-lenders-handbook-m26-7/","https://tenaco.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/VA-Pamphlet-26-7-Revised-Change-43-06-11-24.pdf","https://tenaco.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/VA-Circular-26-25-01-04-02-25.pdf","https://thewbkfirm.com/industry/va-circular-letter-addresses-construction-permanent-loan-requirements","https://acesquality.com/compliance-newshub/news/2018/va-circular-26-18-7-construction-permanent-home-loans","https://compliance.docutech.com/2023/05/16/document-updates-updates-to-va-form-26-1859-cx47","https://regulations.justia.com/regulations/fedreg/2024/04/17/2024-08100.html","https://www.veteransunited.com/valoans/va-construction-loans/","https://www.newrezcorrespondent.com/bulletins/announcement-2019-038-va-handbook-updates-chapters-7-and-10","https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/38/3729"]},

 {k:"va-otc",n:"VA one-time close (single-close) construction-to-permanent",sum:"A VA one-time close is a single loan, closed once before construction, that funds construction draws and then converts to the permanent VA mortgage with no second closing. VA Circular 26-18-7 (now folded into Handbook Chapter 7, Topic 2, revised in 2024) allows it, but each lender decides whether to offer it and under what overlays. Its main advantages are one set of closing costs, one qualification and a rate that can be locked for the whole build.",
  sec:[
   {h:"What a VA one-time close is",b:[
    "Per VA Circular 26-18-7, a one-time close loan is used to close both the construction loan and the permanent financing at the same time.",
    "Funds are released in draws from an escrow-type account, and the lender must get the borrower's written approval before each disbursement.",
    "The loan is underwritten under Chapter 4 of the VA Lenders Handbook, so credit, income, residual income and DTI are examined once, at the front end.",
    "VA guarantees the whole loan, so the veteran's entitlement is used from the start rather than at a later second closing.",
    "The construction loan becomes the permanent mortgage with no new application or second closing; whether a re-verification of employment or credit is done before conversion is a lender overlay: confirm with the AE.",
    "VA Handbook Chapter 7, Topic 2 was revised in its entirety in Change 43 (2024); the old circular is the best public description of the concept, but check the current Topic 2 text for the live rules."]},
   {h:"One-time close versus two-time close",b:[
    "Two-time close: an initial construction loan (typically non-VA) closes first, then a VA permanent loan closes at completion and pays it off, per the circular's definition.",
    "In a two-time close the veteran requalifies at the second closing and pays closing costs twice, and rate risk is not controlled until the permanent loan is locked.",
    "In a one-time close the builder is responsible for construction-period interest unless it is covered by an interest reserve, per the circular; in a two-time close interest on the interim loan is negotiated.",
    "Two-time close is the fallback when no lender offers a VA one-time close, and many national lenders (for example Veterans United publicly) do not originate VA construction loans at all.",
    "The one-time close concentrates the lender's risk (the lender funds draws before the home exists), which is why overlays are heavy and availability is narrow.",
    "A one-time close is a purchase transaction when the veteran contracts with a builder; lenders order the case as new construction and purchase in WebLGY, per a law-firm summary."]},
   {h:"Rate lock mechanics",b:[
    "The main benefit is locking the permanent rate before construction starts and holding it through a 6 to 12 month plus build, per a lender explanation; this must be a lock long enough for the full schedule.",
    "Extended locks are priced as a premium or extension fees; one secondary source says extension costs often run 0.125 to 0.375 percent of the loan amount per 7 to 15 day extension, which is a market claim, not a VA rule.",
    "One lender publishes an extended rate lock of up to 360 days with a one-time float-down within 30 days of closing, which shows how a program can be structured, not what is available to you.",
    "Float-downs are generally one-time, conditional on rates falling by a threshold (often about 0.25 percent per a military.com piece) and may carry a fee; get the lender's written float-down policy.",
    "If the lock expires before the home is complete, the veteran is exposed to the market rate on the extension; this is the largest financial risk in a one-time close.",
    "Ask whether the lender uses a 'lock at closing' for the full construction period or a modification at completion, because the rate-conversion mechanism differs by lender: lender overlay: confirm with the AE."]},
   {h:"Qualification and underwriting",b:[
    "Because there is a single closing, the veteran must qualify for the permanent payment on the full loan amount, plus any housing obligations such as taxes and insurance.",
    "Chapter 4 underwriting applies: a DTI over 41 percent requires compensating factors and residual income analysis, but many one-time close lenders overlay stricter limits.",
    "Typical overlays reported for VA construction: higher minimum FICO than the 580 to 620 seen on ordinary VA loans, lower maximum DTI, a minimum down payment or cash reserves, and an approved-builder list. These vary: lender overlay, confirm with the AE.",
    "Reserves for the interest reserve and contingency are required budget items; the veteran may have to fund contingency in cash or finance it as an allowable acquisition cost.",
    "Income and employment documentation should be fresh at closing and likely again before final conversion; a job change during construction is a risk.",
    "If the veteran is also selling a current home, bridge the timing because the veteran may not need to pay two housing costs through construction."]},
   {h:"Cash to close and fees",b:[
    "The VA funding fee for a construction loan follows the purchase schedule: 2.15 percent first use / 3.3 percent subsequent under 5 percent down, 1.5 percent for 5 to under 10 percent, 1.25 percent for 10 percent or more (secondary sources; confirm at va.gov).",
    "The 2018 circular allowed a lender construction fee up to 2 percent of the loan (if 51 percent or more of proceeds is paid out during construction), in addition to the 1 percent origination charge; verify against current Topic 2.",
    "Cash to close typically includes lot or builder deposits, appraisal fee, title and draw-control fees, inspection fees, prepaids, and any lock extension costs.",
    "Seller (builder) concessions up to 4 percent of reasonable value can cover many veteran costs; the builder typically bears certain interim loan fees under the circular.",
    "Contingency funds are allowed as an acquisition cost, so the veteran can finance some contingency (percentage: lender overlay, confirm with the AE).",
    "Interest reserve: if the loan includes an interest reserve the veteran effectively finances the construction interest, which adds to the loan balance and should be shown in the budget."]},
   {h:"Parties and documents",b:[
    "Parties: veteran, lender, builder/general contractor, title/escrow company (draw control), appraiser, inspector and, if used, the insurer for a ten-year protection plan.",
    "Core documents: contract with builder, plans and specifications, Description of Materials (VA Form 26-1852), cost breakdown/budget, permit, builder financial and license documentation.",
    "VA appraisal request (VA Form 26-1805 in the older forms index; current orders go through WebLGY) producing a Notice of Value on an as-completed basis.",
    "Warranty at completion: VA Form 26-1859 (one-year builder warranty) or a ten-year insured protection plan.",
    "VA Form 26-1880 is the Request for Certificate of Eligibility, so the COE is needed but 26-1880 is not a construction-specific document; if a template lists it for the OTC package, treat it as the COE request only.",
    "Written borrower approval of each draw, lien waivers, inspection or draw reports and any lender-approved change orders form the draw file."]},
   {h:"Appraisal and inspection requirements",b:[
    "The appraisal values the home as if completed per plans and specs; MPRs for proposed construction help ensure it is built to code and HUD requirements.",
    "Inspections are tied to draws: the lender (or its inspector) verifies progress before each disbursement, and Topic 2 includes inspection requirements and project management as revised in 2024.",
    "Historically VA ran compliance inspections; a VA circular says VA stopped these in February 2006 for new and proposed construction in favor of local building inspections and warranties, while an archived Handbook chapter still referenced inspection stages. Confirm what the lender requires.",
    "A final inspection and completion report precede conversion; if work is incomplete, the lender holds funds in escrow, subject to its own rules.",
    "Local building department final inspection and certificate of occupancy are needed before the loan converts to permanent.",
    "Well, septic, termite and soil items are MPR matters (Chapter 12): get lab water tests and septic approvals early."]},
   {h:"Who offers it",b:[
    "Availability is limited; this is a lender-by-lender product, not a VA program lenders must offer.",
    "Large retail lenders often decline VA construction: Veterans United publicly says it does not make VA construction loans for new-home building.",
    "Lenders that do offer a VA one-time close tend to be community banks, credit unions and some mortgage companies with construction departments; I did not find a verified, current list of named VA OTC lenders, so ask AEs for written confirmation.",
    "Several lender marketing pages advertise 'VA one-time close, zero down'; treat claims of 'August 2025 VA launch' or similar as unverified because I could not confirm any new VA program from primary sources.",
    "Wholesale/correspondent investors often publish separate construction manuals; ask for the VA construction supplement."]},
   {h:"Pros and cons versus FHA and conventional one-time close",b:[
    "VA pro: zero down payment is possible, no monthly mortgage insurance, and a single guaranty and funding fee instead of upfront and annual MIP.",
    "VA con: far fewer lenders and heavier overlays than FHA or conventional single-close, which are widely offered (for example, Fannie Mae single-close construction has defined limits such as a construction period of up to 12 months and an overall total of up to 18 months, per a correspondent product profile).",
    "FHA One-Time Close (HUD) has its own documented rules and FICO/down payment structure; compare the veteran's actual numbers rather than assuming VA wins.",
    "Conventional single-close typically needs 5 to 20 percent down and monthly PMI under 20 percent equity, but has broad lender availability.",
    "VA's funding fee can be larger than FHA upfront MIP on a first-use zero-down loan; the VA fee is financed, with no ongoing MI.",
    "Entitlement: a VA one-time close uses entitlement early and for the entire build, which affects any plan to keep or use a second VA loan."]},
   {h:"Top-1 percent tips",b:[
    "Qualify the lender before the builder: one phone call to the AE about VA OTC availability saves weeks.",
    "Get the overlay list, lock extension schedule and float-down policy in writing and price the worst-case extension.",
    "Have the veteran sign a contract that makes the builder responsible for interest and fees the circular assigns to them.",
    "Order the appraisal with complete plans, specs and cost breakdown to avoid revisions.",
    "Build a realistic schedule; add a buffer to the lock period.",
    "Plan the final conversion: certificate of occupancy, completion report, warranty form, final title and lien waiver package ready before the deadline.",
    "Keep a fallback: a two-time close or a builder purchase on a completed spec home."]}
  ],
  steps:[
   "Confirm the lender offers a VA one-time close and obtain the written overlay list and construction manual.",
   "Obtain the COE and confirm entitlement and funding fee status.",
   "Run Chapter 4 underwriting with the full permanent payment, including taxes and insurance.",
   "Vet the builder and collect contract, plans, specs, Description of Materials (VA Form 26-1852), cost breakdown and permits.",
   "Order the as-completed appraisal and review the NOV for conditions.",
   "Agree the budget, including who pays construction interest, interest reserve, contingency and lock extension costs.",
   "Choose and document the rate lock: duration, extension fee schedule and float-down terms.",
   "Issue disclosures and close once; set up the construction escrow and draw schedule with the title company.",
   "Run the draw process: inspection, lien waivers, borrower written approval of each draw, lender approval of change orders.",
   "Monitor lock expiry monthly against the build schedule and request extensions early.",
   "At completion collect certificate of occupancy, final inspection/completion report and VA Form 26-1859 or ten-year plan.",
   "Convert to permanent, confirm first payment date, and report the loan to VA.",
   "Deliver a final walk-through and post-closing file review."],
  ex:[
   {t:"Interest during a 6-month build on a $450,000 one-time close",rows:[
    ["Rate (illustrative)","6.75%"],
    ["Month 1 balance $50,000","$281.25"],
    ["Month 2 balance $120,000","$675.00"],
    ["Month 3 balance $200,000","$1,125.00"],
    ["Month 4 balance $290,000","$1,631.25"],
    ["Month 5 balance $380,000","$2,137.50"],
    ["Month 6 balance $450,000","$2,531.25"],
    ["Total construction interest","$8,381.25"]],
    note:"Monthly interest = balance x 0.0675 / 12. Under the 2018 circular the builder is responsible for this unless it is in an interest reserve, so the contract wording decides who bears the $8,381."},
   {t:"Lock extension exposure on a $450,000 loan",rows:[
    ["Loan amount","$450,000"],
    ["Extension fee low end (0.125%)","$562.50"],
    ["Extension fee high end (0.375%)","$1,687.50"],
    ["Three extensions at high end","$5,062.50"]],
    note:"The 0.125 to 0.375 percent per 7 to 15 day range is a secondary-source market figure, not a VA rule. Ask your lender for its actual schedule."}],
  q:[
   ["Does VA allow a one-time close?","Yes. Circular 26-18-7 defined it and the concept is carried in Handbook Chapter 7, Topic 2 (revised 2024). Each lender decides whether to offer it."],
   ["How does the veteran pay during construction?","The builder is responsible for interest during construction on a one-time close unless covered by an interest reserve, per the circular; the veteran typically starts regular payments after conversion."],
   ["Can the rate be locked for the whole build?","Many lenders offer extended locks; one published program offers up to 360 days with a one-time float-down within 30 days of closing. Details are lender-specific."],
   ["What happens if the lock expires?","The veteran pays an extension fee or takes the market rate. Get the policy in writing; extensions are often priced at 0.125 to 0.375 percent of the loan per extension period (secondary source)."],
   ["Is there a float-down?","Sometimes, usually one time and only if rates fall by a threshold (about 0.25 percent per one source) and often for a fee."],
   ["Is there a second appraisal at conversion?","Generally a completion/final inspection is required; whether a second appraisal is required is lender-specific, so confirm."],
   ["Which VA form is the OTC form?","There is no single OTC form. Relevant forms include 26-1852 (Description of Materials), 26-1805 (appraisal request, older index), 26-1859 (warranty) and the COE request 26-1880 for eligibility."],
   ["Who offers VA OTC?","Mostly community banks, credit unions and specialty mortgage companies; I could not verify a current named list. Veterans United publicly does not offer VA construction."],
   ["How does it compare with FHA OTC?","FHA has wider availability and lower credit requirements but charges upfront and annual MIP; VA allows zero down and no monthly MI but fewer lenders."],
   ["What is the funding fee?","Purchase schedule: 2.15 percent first use under 5 percent down, 3.3 percent subsequent; 1.5 percent at 5 to under 10 percent; 1.25 percent at 10 percent or more; exempt for disability-compensated veterans."],
   ["Can the veteran act as general contractor?","VA permits it per the circular summaries but many lenders prohibit it; if allowed, no builder warranty is provided."],
   ["How long can construction take?","Lenders commonly allow about 12 months; VA text not confirmed here. Fannie Mae single-close references 12 months for the construction period and 18 months total, but that is not VA."],
   ["Can the loan include contingency?","Yes, contingency reserves are an allowable acquisition cost; the percentage requirements were revised in 2024 so confirm with the lender."]],
  caution:[
   "Primary text of Topic 2 as revised in Change 43 (2024) was not retrieved; the specifics here come from the 2018 circular and industry summaries and must be checked against the current Handbook.",
   "The 2018 circular carried a rescission date of April 1, 2021 and was folded into the Handbook; do not cite it as current law.",
   "Rate lock extension and float-down terms are lender products, not VA rules; get the lock agreement in writing.",
   "Claims of a new August 2025 'VA single-close launch' on marketing pages are unverified.",
   "A job change, new debt or credit event during construction can block conversion at a lender that re-verifies; ask the lender.",
   "The builder's financial failure can freeze the project; use title draw control and lien waivers.",
   "Form number confusion: 26-1880 is the COE request, not a construction closing form.",
   "Overlays on FICO, DTI, down payment, builder approvals and veteran-as-builder vary widely: lender overlay, confirm with the AE.",
   "Do not promise a zero-down OTC without confirming the lender's actual overlay."],
  src:["https://www.benefits.va.gov/HOMELOANS/documents/circulars/26_18_7.pdf","https://www.benefits.va.gov/HOMELOANS/documents/circulars/26_18_7_change1.pdf","https://corr.pennymac.com/announcements/18-20-va-circulars-26-18-6-and-26-18-7-loans-for-alteration-and-repairs-construction-financing","https://www.jdsupra.com/legalnews/va-circular-letter-addresses-86758/","https://thewbkfirm.com/industry/va-circular-letter-addresses-construction-permanent-loan-requirements","https://www.tenaco.com/va-revises-topic-2-of-chapter-7-of-lenders-handbook-m26-7/","https://tenaco.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/VA-Pamphlet-26-7-Revised-Change-43-06-11-24.pdf","https://corr.pennymac.com/assets/documents/products/fannie-mae-single-close-construction.pdf","https://www.veteransunited.com/valoans/va-construction-loans/","https://guidelines.townemortgage.com/Products_and_Guidelines/Policy_and_Procedure/Towne_Extended_Lock_Program_28Nov2018.pdf","https://www.military.com/money/va-loans/when-to-lock-your-va-rate.html","https://valoannetwork.com/va-loan-rate-lock/"]},

 {k:"va-rehab",n:"VA rehabilitation, repair and renovation financing",sum:"VA has no product literally called a renovation loan; it guarantees alteration, repair and improvement financing on purchase loans and regular (cash-out) refinances, plus energy-efficiency improvements, under 38 USC 3710 and Lenders Handbook Chapter 7 (Circular 26-18-6 replaced the old A&R topic in 2018). Lenders offer branded 'VA renovation' programs with their own caps and overlays, and VA also allows postponed-repair escrows for minor items. Compared with FHA 203(k) and Fannie HomeStyle, VA is simpler but offered by fewer lenders.",
  sec:[
   {h:"What VA actually permits",b:[
    "VA Circular 26-18-6 (April 2018) replaced Chapter 7, Topic 4 of the Lender's Handbook and lets lenders make VA-guaranteed loans to veterans who want to purchase or refinance a home that needs alteration or repair.",
    "The circular treats alteration, repair, renovation and improvement as interchangeable terms.",
    "Eligible loan types are VA purchase loans and VA regular (cash-out) refinance loans; a cash-out refinance is the route for a veteran who already owns the home.",
    "Proceeds are paid to the builder or contractor during the repair period, and the lender must obtain the borrower's written approval before each draw.",
    "A secondary source says the improvements must be those ordinarily found on similar properties of comparable value in the community and improve basic livability or utility (confirm in the Handbook).",
    "'VA renovation loan' is marketing language; VA's own materials say alteration and repair, so search the Handbook on those terms.",
    "The statutory basis is 38 USC 3710(a) (loan purposes including repairs, alterations and improvements) and the VA loan regulations in 38 CFR Part 36; verify subparagraph citations on Cornell LII."]},
   {h:"Appraisal: subject-to versus as-completed",b:[
    "For alteration and repair loans the appraiser values the home 'as completed' based on a written scope and contractor bids, and the loan is limited by reasonable value.",
    "A 'subject to' appraisal conditions the value on repairs being made; the lender then needs evidence the repairs were done (a completion inspection) before closing, unless an escrow or A&R structure is used.",
    "Appraiser-required repairs (MPR items) must either be done before closing, or, for minor items, handled through a postponed-repair escrow if the lender allows it.",
    "The lender contacts the original VA appraiser for a final inspection of the repairs when work finishes, per a summary of the circular.",
    "As-completed value is only as good as the scope: vague scopes lead to conditioned or reduced values.",
    "Loan amount is the lesser of purchase price plus repairs and the as-completed reasonable value, subject to the lender's cap on repairs."]},
   {h:"Energy-efficiency improvements",b:[
    "VA allows energy-efficiency improvements to be included in a purchase or refinance; the Handbook (Chapter 6 for refinances, Chapter 7 for the use of proceeds) addresses this.",
    "Per a secondary summary, up to $3,000 can be added on documented planned improvements, rising to $6,000 if the projected utility savings cover the extra payment, and more with VA approval (a Certificate of Commitment was cited, which I could not confirm).",
    "Documentation tiers reported by Military Wallet: contractor bids for each improvement; for $3,000 to $6,000 an energy audit and lender approval; above $6,000 VA approval. The monthly payment increase must not exceed the likely monthly utility savings.",
    "Eligible examples: insulation, storm windows and doors, furnace upgrades, heat pumps, solar heating and cooling; appliance replacement with ENERGY STAR units is reportedly not covered.",
    "On an IRRRL the general rule is no cash to the borrower, with one exception: reimbursement for energy-efficiency work up to $6,000 completed within the 90 days before closing (VA Pamphlet 26-7 Chapter 6 excerpt).",
    "Using loan proceeds on an IRRRL for improvements without reimbursing the veteran is a separate option covered in Chapter 7."]},
   {h:"Escrowed repair funds and postponed repairs",b:[
    "Circular 26-18-6 and Handbook Section 9.09 cover escrows for the postponed completion of improvements.",
    "A 2018 Military Times article describes the escrow as withholding 1.5 times the estimated cost of the repairs (estimated by a third party) from the seller's proceeds; that figure and the 90 to 120 day completion window come from news coverage and must be verified in the current Handbook.",
    "Conditions reported: the home must otherwise be move-in ready, and escrow cannot be used for major repairs; the delay must not be unreasonable.",
    "A summary of the circular says the lender may not commingle the escrow with its other funds.",
    "A lender-published program (AFR wholesale VA renovation guidelines, Oct 2025) sets a 15 percent contingency reserve and a six-month completion window and applies leftover escrow to principal unless the borrower funded it at closing; those are that lender's terms, not VA's.",
    "Pennymac's correspondent VA renovation program (2019 launch) required renovations to be complete and escrow closed before delivery, limited the program to delegated purchase transactions, required a 0x30x3 payment history over three months before the renovation and measured seasoning from the 1004D completion date."]},
   {h:"Fees, funding fee and loan structure",b:[
    "A summary of the circular says lenders may charge a construction fee of up to 2 percent if at least 51 percent of proceeds is paid out during the work, and up to 1 percent otherwise (First Tuesday summary; verify against the current Handbook).",
    "The funding fee follows the purchase schedule on a purchase with repairs (2.15 percent first use / 3.3 percent subsequent under 5 percent down; 1.5 percent for 5 to under 10 percent down; 1.25 percent at 10 percent or more), exempt for disability-compensated veterans.",
    "A regular cash-out refinance carries the higher cash-out funding fee schedule; check va.gov for the current table.",
    "Full-entitlement veterans have no VA loan limit; partial-entitlement veterans use the county limit (2026 baseline $832,750, high cost to $1,249,125).",
    "Repair funds are generally financed within the new loan and held in escrow until work is complete.",
    "Underwriting follows Chapter 4, and the repair cost is part of the debt service so the veteran must qualify at the full amount."]},
   {h:"Contractor requirements and the draw process",b:[
    "The work is done by a contractor or builder with a contract; VA allowed disbursement to the contractor, with borrower written approval of each draw.",
    "Lenders commonly require licensed, insured contractors, written bids and a signed contract with a scope of work; these are overlays unless in the Handbook text.",
    "Veteran self-help (doing the work personally) is typically not funded or is limited; lender overlay: confirm with the AE.",
    "Draw requests need an inspection or proof of completion, lien waivers and borrower signature; the final draw follows the completion inspection.",
    "Contingency (a lender overlay in many programs, often 10 to 15 percent) covers overruns; unused amounts are usually applied to principal.",
    "Change orders need lender approval and may require additional funds from the veteran."]},
   {h:"Minimum property requirements and repair triggers",b:[
    "The appraiser must call out conditions that fail MPRs: safe, sound, sanitary and structurally sound with working utilities (Chapter 12).",
    "Common triggers: peeling paint on pre-1978 homes (lead-based paint hazards), roof life under about two years remaining, missing handrails, exposed wiring, inoperable heating, standing water or drainage issues, termite damage.",
    "A wood-destroying insect inspection is required when the appraiser sees evidence or local practice requires it; a failed report requires treatment and repair.",
    "Well and septic systems need water tests and proof they function; failing results are repair items.",
    "MPR repairs can be completed before closing at the seller's or veteran's expense, or through a renovation program or postponed-repair escrow for minor items.",
    "Do not rely on a home inspection; the VA appraisal is required but VA does not require a separate home inspection."]},
   {h:"Seller versus buyer repair negotiation tactics",b:[
    "Write the offer so the seller must complete MPR repairs identified by the appraisal, or the buyer can cancel; add an escrow addendum as an alternative.",
    "A VA 'option to terminate' (VA Amendatory Clause) lets the veteran back out if the appraised value comes in below price; use it to negotiate repairs and price.",
    "Seller concessions up to 4 percent of reasonable value can be used for the veteran's costs but cannot be used to cure property defects in a way that hides them from the appraisal; keep repair credits and concessions separated in the contract.",
    "If the seller will not repair, move the repair into a renovation structure: the seller keeps the price, the veteran finances the work.",
    "For minor repairs ask the lender whether the postponed-repair escrow is available so closing is not delayed.",
    "Obtain contractor bids before offering so the repair credit or price reduction is based on real numbers."]},
   {h:"Comparison with FHA 203(k) and Fannie Mae HomeStyle",b:[
    "FHA 203(k) is a HUD program with a Limited (streamlined) and Standard version, consultant requirements and upfront plus annual MIP; check HUD Handbook 4000.1 for limits.",
    "Fannie Mae HomeStyle Renovation is a conventional product with 5 percent minimum down in many cases, PMI, and renovation funds up to a percentage of the as-completed value (check the Selling Guide for current limits).",
    "VA advantage: potential 100 percent financing, no monthly mortgage insurance, and no separate consultant requirement in the sources reviewed.",
    "VA disadvantage: fewer lenders offer renovation, and overlays (caps, contingency, time windows) vary; FHA and HomeStyle are more widely available.",
    "VA funding fee can be financed; FHA MIP and conventional PMI continue monthly, so total cost depends on the veteran's equity and credit.",
    "Large or structural projects often fit 203(k) Standard or HomeStyle better than a VA program with a low cap."]},
   {h:"Common failures and what to tell the veteran",b:[
    "The appraisal comes in below price plus repairs and the veteran must bring cash or renegotiate.",
    "The contractor underbids and runs out of funds; insist on a contingency and a fixed-price contract.",
    "The project exceeds the lender's time window and the escrow is applied to principal.",
    "The veteran assumes any improvement qualifies; luxury items such as a pool may be excluded under the 'ordinarily found' standard.",
    "Lenders do not accept a veteran as their own contractor.",
    "Tell the veteran: VA guarantees the loan, the lender sets the renovation rules, and the work must be done by a licensed contractor within the lender's timeline."]}
  ],
  steps:[
   "Confirm the lender offers a VA renovation/alteration-and-repair product and get its caps, contingency, time window and overlay list in writing.",
   "Obtain the COE, confirm entitlement and the funding fee.",
   "Determine the path: purchase with repairs, cash-out refinance with improvements, energy-efficiency add-on, or a minor postponed-repair escrow.",
   "Have the veteran get written bids from licensed contractors with a defined scope of work.",
   "Verify the contractor: license, insurance, references.",
   "Order the appraisal 'as completed' with the scope and bids supplied to the appraiser.",
   "Review the NOV for MPR conditions and decide which repairs are done before closing and which are funded.",
   "Underwrite under Chapter 4 including the full loan amount with repair funds.",
   "Close and set up the repair escrow, kept separate from the lender's funds.",
   "Run draws: written borrower approval, inspection or proof of completion, lien waivers.",
   "On completion, order the final inspection (1004D or equivalent) by the original appraiser.",
   "Release the final draw, apply any unused funds per the lender rule, and collect the final documentation.",
   "Deliver the loan to the investor only after completion if the program requires it."],
  ex:[
   {t:"Purchase with $40,000 of repairs, first-time VA use, no down payment",rows:[
    ["Purchase price","$300,000"],
    ["Repair budget including contingency","$40,000"],
    ["Price plus repairs","$340,000"],
    ["As-completed appraised value","$350,000"],
    ["Base loan (lesser of 340,000 and 350,000)","$340,000"],
    ["Funding fee 2.15% x 340,000","$7,310"],
    ["Total loan","$347,310"],
    ["Cushion remaining in value","$10,000"]],
    note:"If the as-completed value had been $335,000 the loan would be capped near the value, leaving the veteran to cover the $5,000 gap plus fee considerations. The funding fee here is the zero-down first-use rate."},
   {t:"Energy add-on payment test and postponed-repair escrow",rows:[
    ["Energy improvement amount","$6,000"],
    ["Rate and term (illustrative)","6.5% / 30 years"],
    ["Payment factor per $1,000","about 6.32"],
    ["Added payment 6.0 x 6.32","about $37.92 per month"],
    ["Savings needed on utilities","at least $37.92 per month"],
    ["Postponed-repair estimate","$8,000"],
    ["Escrow at 1.5x (news-reported factor)","$12,000"]],
    note:"The energy test compares added payment to projected utility savings. The 1.5x escrow factor is from 2018 news coverage; verify in Handbook Section 9.09 and the lender's rules."}],
  q:[
   ["Does VA have a renovation loan?","Not under that name. VA guarantees alteration, repair and improvement financing on purchase and regular refinance loans (Circular 26-18-6, Handbook Chapter 7), and lenders brand their own VA renovation programs."],
   ["Can a veteran finance repairs on a purchase?","Yes, proceeds are paid to the contractor during the repair period with the borrower's written approval for each draw."],
   ["Can a veteran who owns the home finance improvements?","Yes through a VA regular (cash-out) refinance; an IRRRL generally cannot give cash, except for energy improvements within 90 days before closing up to $6,000."],
   ["What energy improvements qualify?","Insulation, storm windows and doors, heating system upgrades, heat pumps and solar heating/cooling are typical examples; appliance swaps are reportedly not covered."],
   ["How much can be added for energy work?","Secondary sources say $3,000 with documentation, up to $6,000 if savings cover the payment, and more with VA approval; confirm in the Handbook."],
   ["What does the appraiser require?","As-completed value with scope and bids, and MPR repairs must be fixed or escrowed."],
   ["Can the seller do the repairs?","Yes, the contract can require seller repairs, and the veteran can use the VA amendatory clause if value or repair outcomes fail."],
   ["What is the postponed-repair escrow?","An escrow for minor repairs finished after closing; news coverage reports 1.5x the estimate, a 90 to 120 day window and no major repairs. Verify against Section 9.09."],
   ["Is there a repair cap?","VA text did not specify a cap in the sources reviewed; lenders set caps and overlays."],
   ["How does the funding fee work?","Same as a purchase with repairs; cash-out refinance has its own higher schedule; exempt for disability-compensated veterans."],
   ["VA versus 203(k)?","VA offers zero down and no MI but is less available; 203(k) has broader availability and a consultant structure with MIP."],
   ["VA versus HomeStyle?","HomeStyle is conventional, needs down payment and PMI, and is widely offered; VA is cheaper monthly if the lender offers it."],
   ["Can the veteran do the work?","Usually lenders require a licensed contractor; self-help is typically not funded (lender overlay)."],
   ["What if the work runs late?","Time windows are lender-specific (for example six months in one wholesale program) and unused funds may go to principal."]],
  caution:[
   "The Circular 26-18-6 text was not retrieved; points come from summaries and must be checked against the current Handbook.",
   "The 1.5x escrow factor and 90 to 120 day window are from 2018 news coverage, not confirmed primary text.",
   "Construction fee caps (up to 2 percent / 1 percent) come from a secondary summary; confirm before quoting.",
   "Lender caps, contingency, time windows and contractor rules are overlays: confirm with the AE.",
   "Energy-efficiency amounts and the Certificate of Commitment process were not verified from primary text.",
   "A 'subject to' appraisal requires proof of completion; do not close before the repair condition is cleared unless an escrow structure applies.",
   "Do not mix seller concessions with repair credits without checking the 4 percent cap and the appraisal.",
   "Pre-1978 homes: lead-based paint repairs are a frequent surprise.",
   "A VA regular refinance with improvements is cash-out and may carry a higher funding fee and LTV limits; compare to alternatives."],
  src:["https://www.benefits.va.gov/HOMELOANS/documents/circulars/26_18_6.pdf","https://tenaco.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/05/VA-Circular-26-18-6.pdf","https://journal.firsttuesday.us/lender-guidance-for-va-home-improvement-mortgages/64840/","https://www.militarytimes.com/home-hq/2018/02/05/va-escrow-and-repairs-a-rule-that-may-speed-your-home-buying-process/","https://www.militarytimes.com/home-hq/va-loan-center/2018/02/20/how-vas-energy-efficiency-program-could-save-you-cash-on-home-repairs-utility-bills/","https://www.tenaco.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/VA-Pamphlet-26-7-Chapter-6-Refinancing-Loans-11-01-24.pdf","https://corr.pennymac.com/announcements/19-16-announcement-2-25-2019","https://corr.pennymac.com/assets/documents/products/va-renovation-product-profile.pdf","https://www.elend.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Program-Guidelines_VA-Renovation-Program_Wholesale_10132025-Rebrand.pdf","https://themilitarywallet.com/complete-guide-to-the-va-energy-efficient-mortgage/","https://valoannetwork.com/va-water-wood-rules-well-septic-termite-checklist/","https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/38/3710"]}
]);
